<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bolsa Família</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2003">2003</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bolsa Família is a Brazilian social programme that pays monthly cash transfers to poor families on condition that their children attend school and are vaccinated. Created in 2003 under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luiz-inacio-lula-da-silva">Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva</a> by merging earlier schemes, it reached about a quarter of the population and is credited with sharply reducing extreme poverty. Renamed Auxílio Brasil under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jair-bolsonaro">Jair Bolsonaro</a>, it was restored under its old name in 2023.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bolsa-familia.jpg" alt="Bolsa Família" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Secretaria de Comunicação Social / Governo Federal do Brasil · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Logo_Bolsa_Fam%C3%ADlia_2023.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The forerunner was Bolsa Escola, a payment conditional only on school attendance, pioneered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brasilia">Brasília</a> by its governor Cristovam Buarque and copied by other municipalities and states. In 2001 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-henrique-cardoso">Fernando Henrique Cardoso</a> made it federal. In 2003 Lula joined Bolsa Escola with Bolsa Alimentação, Cartão Alimentação and Auxílio Gás, all parts of Cardoso's Rede de Proteção Social, and created a new Ministry for Social Development and Confronting Hunger to run it. The merger cut administrative costs and simplified matters for families and officials. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s Oportunidades had been the first nationwide programme of the kind.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The money went preferably to the female head of a household, through a Citizen Card working like a debit card and issued by the state savings bank Caixa Econômica Federal; the names of recipients and the sums paid were published online. Municipal governments acted as the federal government's agents, bypassing the state governors. A family that failed the school attendance rule five times in a row lost its benefit. In 2006 the cost was estimated at about 0.5 per cent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a>, covering about 11.2 million families, and in 2011 the programme reached 26 per cent of the population. The programme is reputed to have helped Lula win the 2006 election.</p><p>On 30 December 2021 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jair-bolsonaro">Jair Bolsonaro</a> signed into law Auxílio Brasil, formally ending Bolsa Família. After his re-election in 2022 Lula restored the old name, and the second version was launched in 2023 with a promise of transfers of at least 600 reais.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Opponents claimed that the programme discouraged work and bought the votes of the poor, and Lula's critics accused it of using revenues of the CPMF tax at the expense of public health. A World Bank official, Bénédicte de la Brière, found that the transfers did not reduce adult work. A UNDP study found that over 80 per cent of the benefits went to families in poverty and credited the programme with about 20 per cent of the fall in inequality since 2001, and a 2018 study linked wider coverage with lower suicide rates between 2004 and 2012.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bolsa Família” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva, president from 2003 to 2010, expanded social programmes such as <b>Bolsa Família</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-republic-brazil">New Republic (Brazil)</a></li></ul></section>
